(: ===================================================================
   extract_high_accuracy.xpath
   ---------------------------------------------------------------
   XPath expressions for extracting runs by performance.
   Target document: xml/samples/sample_experiment.xml
   (root element <Experiment>, runs under Runs/Run).

   Each expression is standalone. Run one at a time with an XPath
   engine, or via the demo in tests/test_queries.py.
   =================================================================== :)

(: 1. The brief's headline example, adapted to this schema:
      all high-accuracy runs (accuracy > 0.9). :)
//Run[metric = 'accuracy' and value > 0.9]

(: 2. The dataset NAMES that achieved accuracy > 0.9
      (the brief's //Run[...]/Dataset, here Dataset is a child element). :)
//Run[metric = 'accuracy' and value > 0.9]/Dataset/text()

(: 3. All runs for a specific algorithm via its reference id. :)
//Run[@algorithmRef = 'algo_GradientBoosting']

(: 4. Regression runs with a strong fit (R^2 >= 0.8). :)
//Run[metric = 'r2' and value >= 0.8]

(: 5. Names of all binary-classification datasets. :)
//Dataset[@task = 'binary']/@name

(: 6. Count how many runs exceeded accuracy 0.9. :)
count(//Run[metric = 'accuracy' and value > 0.9])

(: 7. Datasets whose feature-to-instance ratio is high (> 0.05):
      wide, small-sample problems. :)
//Dataset[MetaFeatures/feature_to_instance_ratio > 0.05]/@name

(: 8. The value of every GradientBoosting run on a given dataset. :)
//Run[@algorithmRef = 'algo_GradientBoosting'
      and @datasetRef = 'ds_balance_scale']/value/text()
